// **All three of `modules/GL`'s per-OS library declarations, checked on whatever host runs this**
// (ADR-0184 §5).
//
// # Why this file exists
//
// `modules/GL` picks its OpenGL library at compile time, and on any one machine exactly **one** of its three
// branches runs. So the Linux and Windows declarations were text that no test could ever look at — and two of
// three branches permanently unexecuted is not a portability claim, it is a hope. That is precisely the shape
// AGENTS.md warns about under "if a construct is legal in the corpus, something must execute or snapshot it".
//
// So `gl_library_for` takes the OS as an **argument**, and `gl_library` is `gl_library_for(os())`. The choice
// and the mapping are separated, and the mapping is what this file exercises: all three strings, on macOS, on
// Linux, on Windows, from one host.
//
// # What it asserts, and why each part earns its line
//
//   * **Each string is exactly right**, compared character for character through `String.equal`, because `==`
//     on a `string` is E0278 by design (ADR-0099 §4) — two strings could compare as bytes or as pointers and
//     Jairs declines to pick. A `contains`-style check would pass on `"gl :: #system_library \"GLU\";"`.
//   * **macOS differs in its *directive*, not just its name** — `#framework` against `#system_library`. That is
//     the whole reason `jr-link` needed a second argument form (ADR-0183), so a test that only compared
//     library *names* would miss the interesting half entirely.
//   * **The three are pairwise different.** This catches the mistake a reader would actually make here:
//     editing one branch and leaving another accidentally aliased to the same text. Two branches returning one
//     string is a plausible-looking module and a program that links against the wrong library on one platform.
//   * **The host's own choice agrees with the mapping** — `gl_library()` equals `gl_library_for(os())` for
//     this machine. It is the one bit that ties the tested mapping back to what the module actually compiles
//     with, and without it the mapping could be correct while the `#insert` used something else.
//
// Every comparison runs in **both** engines: these are string equalities over compile-time-known text, so
// there is nothing here for the VM and a native binary to disagree about — which is exactly why this belongs
// in `valid/` rather than as an integration test. The *linking* claim needs a linker and lives in
// `crates/jr-cli/tests/integration.rs`; the *mapping* claim does not, and gets checked on every engine on
// every run instead.
//
// Exit code 15 = 1 + 2 + 4 + 8, so a single failing group names itself.
#import "Basic";
GL :: #import "GL";
Str :: #import "String";

main :: () {
    n := 0;

    // macOS is a framework — a different directive, not merely a different name.
    if Str.equal(GL.gl_library_for(Operating_System.MACOS), "gl :: #framework \"OpenGL\";") {
        n = n + 1;
    }

    // Linux is an ordinary `-lGL`.
    if Str.equal(GL.gl_library_for(Operating_System.LINUX), "gl :: #system_library \"GL\";") {
        n = n + 2;
    }

    // Windows is the ordinary form under a different name.
    if Str.equal(GL.gl_library_for(Operating_System.WINDOWS), "gl :: #system_library \"opengl32\";") {
        n = n + 4;
    }

    // The three must be pairwise distinct, and the host's own choice must be one of them — the bit that ties
    // the mapping to what the module really compiled against.
    mac := GL.gl_library_for(Operating_System.MACOS);
    linux := GL.gl_library_for(Operating_System.LINUX);
    windows := GL.gl_library_for(Operating_System.WINDOWS);
    distinct := !Str.equal(mac, linux) && !Str.equal(linux, windows) && !Str.equal(mac, windows);

    if distinct && Str.equal(GL.gl_library(), GL.gl_library_for(os())) {
        n = n + 8;
    }

    exit(n);
}
